\chapter{Overview of helper functions and methods}

We will now document some possible API calls to LADOK.
We also document the tests of these functions to illustrate their use.


\section{Example outputs and test code for the API}

We will provide both example outputs and test code.
The test code is the test cases we want to be able to run with [[pytest]] 
later.
We will design the test functions in such a way that we can reuse them to show 
the example outputs from the API requests.

We will use the following to test the API methods.
Each test that talks to LADOK takes the session as its [[ladok]] argument,
a fixture that reuses the cached test-environment session through
[[ladok3.test_session]] (see \cref{Testing}).
Running the API tests against LADOK then requires no MFA at all when the
cache is warm.
By default, though, the tests don't talk to LADOK: they replay the
responses recorded in [[tests/cassettes/test_api]], which is what the
[[vcr]] mark on every test in the file asks for.
The few tests that send no request at all, such as those of our own
validation and pagination, take [[offline_ladok]] instead, a session that
never logs in.
