\subsection{GET, PUT and POST queries}

To make things easier, we will add four methods: [[get_query]], [[put_query]], 
[[post_query]] and [[del_query]], which are shortcuts to make GET, PUT, POST 
and DEL queries to LADOK.

We note that we must record the time of the last GET request to track the 
freshness of the XSRF token.
The other requests track that by the use of the XSRF token itself.
We'll get back to this in \cref{XSRFtoken}.

A successful HTTP status does not guarantee that we actually received LADOK
JSON. When an authenticated session expires, LADOK may redirect us to an HTML
login page and still finish with [[200 OK]]. If we were to call
[[response.json()]] unconditionally in that situation, the user would only see
a low-level decoding failure. We therefore decode successful responses through a
small helper that raises [[LadokAPIError]] with the final URL and content type
when the body is not JSON.

The raw-text case needs one extra wrinkle.  The [[weblogin]] session wrapper is
helpful for JSON endpoints because it can inspect HTML responses and trigger a
login flow when LADOK silently redirects us.  But the same wrapper also feeds
all text responses to its HTML detectors, and XML with an encoding declaration
causes [[lxml.html]] to raise before our code gets the body.  We therefore let
[[get_query_text]] force authentication with an ordinary JSON request first,
then make the raw request through [[requests.Session.request(self.session,
...)]]: that still uses the 
same authenticated [[self.session]] object (same cookies, same MFA-backed
login), but bypasses [[AutologinSession.request]]'s HTML detection layer.
<<functions>>=
def response_json_or_error(
    response: requests.Response, path: str, method: str) -> dict | list:
  """Return JSON from [[response]] or raise contextual [[LadokAPIError]]."""
  try:
    return response.json()
  except ValueError as err:
    content_type = response.headers.get("content-type", "<missing>")
    raise LadokAPIError(
      f"{method} request to {path} returned non-JSON response "
      f"from {response.url} (Content-Type: {content_type}): "
      f"{compact_body(response.text)}") from err
@

A whole HTML page in an error message buries the one line that matters, so
we squeeze its whitespace and cut it short.
<<functions>>=
def compact_body(text: str, limit: int = 120) -> str:
  """Return `text` on one line, shortened to at most `limit` characters."""
  body = " ".join(text.split())
  if len(body) > limit:
    body = body[:limit - 3] + "..."
  return body
@

Let's verify the improved error reporting with a synthetic HTML login page. The
helper should raise [[LadokAPIError]] rather than leaking a bare JSON decoding
exception.
<<test functions>>=
def test_response_json_or_error_reports_html_login_pages() -> None:
  class Response:
    url = "https://start.ladok.se/gui/loggain"
    headers = {"content-type": "text/html;charset=UTF-8"}
    text = "<html><title>Ladok - start</title></html>"

    def json(self):
      raise ValueError("not json")

  try:
    ladok3.response_json_or_error(Response(), "/studentinformation", "GET")
  except ladok3.LadokAPIError as err:
    assert "non-JSON response" in str(err)
    assert "text/html;charset=UTF-8" in str(err)
    assert "https://start.ladok.se/gui/loggain" in str(err)
  else:
    assert False
@

\subsubsection{Reporting a failed request}\label{QueryErrors}

The helper above deals with a successful status that carries the wrong kind
of body; the query methods must also report requests that fail outright.
When LADOK refuses a request, it usually explains why in the
[[Meddelande]] field of a JSON body, and that explanation is what the user
needs to see.
But not every failure carries such a body.
The requests that sent the wrong XSRF token (\cref{XSRFtoken}), for
instance, got an HTML \enquote{Application error} page with status 500.
Since our retry policy hands such final responses back to us
(\cref{RetryPolicy}), we must expect them here.
An earlier version put the whole response body into the message, which for
such a page meant a screenful of HTML.
We therefore use LADOK's own message when there is one, and otherwise the
status and a shortened body.
The two cases also differ in kind.
With a [[Meddelande]], LADOK understood the request and refused it, which is
what [[LadokServerError]] stands for (\cref{ExceptionClasses}); without one,
we only know that the request failed, a plain [[LadokAPIError]].

All five query methods below fail the same way, so they share this helper.
It returns the exception rather than raising it, so that each method ends
with an explicit [[raise]] and the reader sees where its control flow stops.
<<functions>>=
def query_error(response: requests.Response, path: str,
                method: str) -> LadokAPIError:
  """Return an exception describing the failed `response`.

  If the body is LADOK JSON with an error message (`Meddelande`), returns a
  `LadokServerError` with that message. Otherwise returns a `LadokAPIError`
  with the HTTP status and a shortened body.
  """
  failed = f"{method} request to {path} failed"
  try:
    message = response.json()["Meddelande"]
  except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
    return LadokAPIError(f"{failed}: {response.status_code} {response.reason}: "
                         f"{compact_body(response.text)}")
  return LadokServerError(f"{failed}: {message}")
@

Let's verify both kinds of failure: an HTML error page from the proxy and a
JSON refusal from LADOK.
<<test functions>>=
class FakeFailedResponse:
  def __init__(self, status_code, reason, text, json_data=None):
    self.status_code = status_code
    self.reason = reason
    self.text = text
    self.__json_data = json_data

  def json(self):
    if self.__json_data is None:
      raise ValueError("not json")
    return self.__json_data

def test_query_error_shortens_html_error_pages() -> None:
  page = ("<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n"
          "<title>Ladok - Application error</title>\n"
          + "<meta>\n" * 100 + "</head></html>")
  err = ladok3.query_error(
    FakeFailedResponse(500, "Internal Server Error", page),
    "/resultat/internal/kurstillfalle/moment", "PUT")
  assert type(err) is ladok3.LadokAPIError
  assert "PUT request to /resultat/internal/kurstillfalle/moment" in str(err)
  assert "500 Internal Server Error" in str(err)
  assert "Application error" in str(err)
  assert "\n" not in str(err)
  assert len(str(err)) < 250

def test_query_error_uses_ladok_message() -> None:
  err = ladok3.query_error(
    FakeFailedResponse(400, "Bad Request", '{"Meddelande": "..."}',
                       {"Meddelande": "Resultatet är redan attesterat"}),
    "/resultat/internal/resultat/uppdatera/1", "PUT")
  assert isinstance(err, ladok3.LadokServerError)
  assert isinstance(err, ladok3.LadokAPIError)
  assert str(err).endswith("failed: Resultatet är redan attesterat")
