\subsubsection{Which XSRF token is ours}

A fresh token is not enough; it must also be the token of the right LADOK
environment.
Each environment sets its own [[XSRF-TOKEN]] cookie, scoped to its own host:
[[start.ladok.se]] for production and [[start.test.ladok.se]] for the test
environment.
Both cookies can end up in the same cookie jar.
That is no accident: [[ladok3.test_session]] shares the production session's
underlying session, and with it the cookie jar, so that the test environment
can log in on the production SSO cookies (\cref{CachedSession}).
That shared jar is then pickled into the cache, so once a test run or a
documentation build has touched the test environment, every later
production session restored from the cache carries both tokens.

Contrast two ways of reading the token from such a jar.
An earlier version flattened the jar into a dictionary with
[[get_dict()]].
A dictionary has room for only one [[XSRF-TOKEN]], so the cookie that the jar
lists last wins.
The jar lists its cookies sorted by domain, and [[start.test.ladok.se]] sorts
after [[start.ladok.se]], so the test environment's token always won.
A production session then sent the test environment's token with every PUT,
POST and DELETE request, and LADOK answered each of them with an
\enquote{Application error} (status 500).
GET requests carry no token, so they kept working, which made the problem
look like a fault in a particular LADOK endpoint rather than in our session.

Instead we ask the jar for the cookie that our own host set.
This works because the jar records the domain of every cookie, and LADOK sets
its token on the bare host name, without a leading dot, so the domain matches
the host of [[self.base_url]] exactly.
We derive the host inside the property rather than storing it in the
constructor:
sessions restored from the cache are unpickled, not constructed, so an
attribute that only the constructor sets would be missing on them.
We deliberately leave out the path.
Should our host ever set a second token on another path, the jar raises
[[CookieConflictError]] instead of silently choosing one of them, and a loud
failure is what we want for an ambiguous token.
If our host has set no token at all, we tell the user to log in again, rather
than failing with a bare [[KeyError]].
<<return the XSRF token of our own LADOK environment>>=
host = urllib.parse.urlparse(self.base_url).hostname
token = self.session.cookies.get("XSRF-TOKEN", domain=host)
if token is None:
  raise LadokAPIError(
    f"no XSRF token from {host}; log in again with `ladok login`")
return token
@

Let's verify that a production session and a test-environment session that
share one cookie jar each get their own token.
We set both tokens the way LADOK does and mark both sessions as recently used,
so that the freshness check makes no request and the test runs offline.
With [[get_dict()]], the production session would get the test token here.
<<test functions>>=
def make_sessions_sharing_one_jar():
  prod = ladok3.LadokSession("KTH Royal Institute of Technology")
  test = ladok3.LadokSession("KTH Royal Institute of Technology",
                             test_environment=True,
                             session=prod.session)
  for session in (prod, test):
    session._LadokSession__access_time = datetime.datetime.now()
  return prod, test

def test_xsrf_token_comes_from_own_environment() -> None:
  prod, test = make_sessions_sharing_one_jar()
  prod.session.cookies.set("XSRF-TOKEN", "prod-token",
                           domain="start.ladok.se", path="/gui")
  prod.session.cookies.set("XSRF-TOKEN", "test-token",
                           domain="start.test.ladok.se", path="/gui")
  assert prod.xsrf_token == "prod-token"
  assert test.xsrf_token == "test-token"
