\section{Cleaning data for printing}

We sometimes want to print the data, for instance, example output in this 
document.
For this reason we introduce some cleaning functions.
These recursively transcends the JSON structure removing the data that should 
be removed.
<<functions>>=
def clean_data(json_obj: dict | list) -> dict | list:
  """
  Clean a JSON object by removing internal links and pseudonymizing personal data.

  Args:
      json_obj (dict or list): The JSON data to clean.

  Returns:
      dict or list: The cleaned JSON object.
  """
  remove_links(json_obj)
  pseudonymize(json_obj)
  return json_obj
@

The [[remove_links]] functions removes the [[link]] key--value pairs.
The [[link]] values contains URLs for all requests that data are based on.
<<functions>>=
def remove_links(json_obj: dict | list) -> None:
  """
  Recursively removes all "link" keys and values
  """
  if isinstance(json_obj, dict):
    if "link" in json_obj:
      json_obj.pop("link")
    for key, value in json_obj.items():
      remove_links(value)
  elif isinstance(json_obj, list):
    for item in json_obj:
      remove_links(item)
@

The [[pseudonymize]] function replaces the data that identify or describe a
person with dummy entries.
Most of the fields it covers describe the student: name, personnummer, date
of birth, sex, whether the student is alive, and contact details.
Two more fields are easy to miss precisely because they do \emph{not} describe
the student.
LADOK stamps most records with audit metadata, and [[SenastAndradAv]]
(\enquote{last changed by}) names the \emph{staff member} who last touched the
record, as their institutional email address---an audit of the rendered
example outputs in this documentation found real staff addresses leaking
through it.
Pseudonymization has to protect staff as well as students, so we replace it
like any other email address.
Examination occasions carry a similar field, [[SkapadAv]] (\enquote{created
by}), with the username of the staff member who created the occasion.
Similarly, the delivery-preference records use the bare key [[Epost]] where
student records use [[Epostadress]], so we must cover both spellings.

Result records name people in three more places.
[[Beslutsfattare]] (\enquote{decision maker}) is the examiner who decided
the result, and [[OvrigaMedverkande]] (\enquote{other contributors}) is a
newline-separated list of the teachers who took part in the grading.
[[Anteckning]] (\enquote{note}) is free text, found on suspensions and
results, and free text can name anyone, so we replace it wholesale rather
than trying to find names in it.
The same goes for the free text of an examination occasion,
[[OvrigInformation]] (\enquote{other information}).
When we report a result, we send [[Beslutsfattare]] as a list of UIDs (see
[[finalize_result_JSON]]), but LADOK returns it as the examiner's name.
We therefore replace only the string form and keep a list of UIDs, which
carries no name.
[[Attestant]] is whoever certified (attested) the result in LADOK, who need
not be the one who decided it.
It comes in two shapes too:
result histories give a record, whose name fields the rules above already
replace, but attestation views give a plain string with the name.
So we replace its string form in the same way.

Where there is no examiner to name, LADOK puts its own text in these
fields, \enquote{Uppgift om examinator saknas/Not applicable}.
That text names no one, and a dummy name in its place would show an
examiner where LADOK says there is none, so we keep it.

The function only knows the keys it lists.
If LADOK adds a field that names a person, that field goes through
untouched until someone adds it here.
This is why the documentation's example outputs are scanned before we publish
them (the [[check-doc-privacy]] target in the top-level [[Makefile]]):
the scan counts the distinct values per key, so a new key that carries
names stands out even though this function doesn't know about it.
The count has two blind spots, though.
It only helps when the examples show several people:
an example about one student gives each of that student's fields one value,
just as a pseudonym has one value.
And it counts only text values, so a number or a truth value, such as the
student's sex ([[KonID]]) or whether the student is deceased
([[Avliden]]), never shows up in it at all.
So every field that describes a person must be listed here, whether or not
the scan would notice it.

We keep the replacements in a table, so a new key is a one-line change.
The names are deliberately unrealistic (\enquote{Studentzadeh}) and the
personnummer and date of birth are the fictive Tolvan's, so that no one
mistakes the documentation's examples for real records.
The sex becomes the neutral 0 and every student is alive:
neither belongs in an example.
<<constants>>=
PSEUDONYMS = {
  "Fornamn": "Student",
  "Efternamn": "Studentzadeh",
  "Personnummer": "19121212-1212",
  "Fodelsedata": "1912-12-12",
  "KonID": 0,
  "Avliden": False,
  "Epostadress": "user@domain.se",
  "Epost": "user@domain.se",
  "Anvandarnamn": "user@domain.se",
  "Utdelningsadress": "Stora vägen 1",
  "Postnummer": "12345",
  "Postort": "Byn",
  "Telefonnummer": "0701234567",
  "SenastAndradAv": "user@domain.se",
  "SkapadAv": "user@domain.se",
  "Anteckning": "Anteckning",
  "OvrigInformation": "Övrig information",
  "OvrigaMedverkande": "Medarbetare",
}

PSEUDONYMS_IF_STRING = {
  "Beslutsfattare": "Examinator",
  "Attestant": "Examinator",
}
NO_EXAMINER_TEXTS = {"Uppgift om examinator saknas/Not applicable"}
@

Two keys say something about a person by being there at all:
[[Skyddsstatus]] marks a protected identity (\enquote{sekretessmarkering})
and [[FolkbokforingsbevakningTillOchMed]] says that LADOK watches the
population register for changes to the student.
A dummy value would still show that the record has the key, so we remove
these keys instead, as [[remove_links]] does with the links.
<<constants>>=
REMOVED_KEYS = ["Skyddsstatus", "FolkbokforingsbevakningTillOchMed"]
<<functions>>=
def pseudonymize(json_obj: dict | list) -> None:
  """
  Recursively pseudonymizes a JSON data record in place.

  Replaces the values of the keys in `PSEUDONYMS` (names, personnummer,
  date of birth, sex, contact details, free text) and the string values of
  the keys in `PSEUDONYMS_IF_STRING`, and removes the keys in
  `REMOVED_KEYS`.  UIDs are kept.
  """
  if isinstance(json_obj, dict):
    for key in REMOVED_KEYS:
      json_obj.pop(key, None)
    for key, pseudonym in PSEUDONYMS.items():
      if key in json_obj:
        json_obj[key] = pseudonym
    for key, pseudonym in PSEUDONYMS_IF_STRING.items():
      value = json_obj.get(key)
      if isinstance(value, str) and value not in NO_EXAMINER_TEXTS:
        json_obj[key] = pseudonym
    for value in json_obj.values():
      pseudonymize(value)
  elif isinstance(json_obj, list):
    for item in json_obj:
      pseudonymize(item)
@

Let's verify that both keys are removed, also from nested records, and that
nothing else in the record is touched.
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("key", ["Skyddsstatus",
                                 "FolkbokforingsbevakningTillOchMed"])
def test_pseudonymize_removes(key) -> None:
  data = {key: "2027-01-01", "Rattigheter": [{key: "SEKRETESS"}]}
  ladok3.pseudonymize(data)
  assert data == {"Rattigheter": [{}]}
@

Let's verify that every field we promise to replace is replaced, also when
the record is nested.
The observed leaks were in nested records: [[SenastAndradAv]] inside a
\emph{list} of Rättigheter records, so we put each field both at the top
level and inside a list of records.
We list the fields here independently of [[PSEUDONYMS]]: a test that looped
over the table itself would still pass if someone deleted an entry from it.
It is not enough that the value changes, since a transformation of it, say
the name spelt backwards, would also be a change.
We therefore pseudonymize two different people and require the same result
for both: then nothing of the original can remain.
<<test functions>>=
PSEUDONYMIZED_KEYS = [
  "Fornamn", "Efternamn", "Personnummer", "Epostadress", "Epost",
  "Anvandarnamn", "Utdelningsadress", "Postnummer", "Postort",
  "Telefonnummer", "SenastAndradAv", "Anteckning", "OvrigaMedverkande",
  "Beslutsfattare", "Fodelsedata", "KonID", "Avliden", "SkapadAv",
  "OvrigInformation", "Attestant",
]

@pytest.mark.parametrize("key", PSEUDONYMIZED_KEYS)
def test_pseudonymize_replaces(key) -> None:
  first = {key: "Anna Andersson", "Rattigheter": [{key: "Anna Andersson"}]}
  second = {key: "Bo Berg", "Rattigheter": [{key: "Bo Berg"}]}
  ladok3.pseudonymize(first)
  ladok3.pseudonymize(second)
  assert first[key] not in ("Anna Andersson", "Bo Berg")
  assert first == second
