\section{[[participants_JSON]]}\label{ParticipantsJSON}

The method returns JSON data containing a list of students (the participants in 
the course round).
(This is an extension of Maguire's original [[participants_JSON]] method.
The essential difference is keyword arguments to filter which students to 
include.)

LADOK filters the list by the participants' [[deltagaretillstand]], a list of
codes.
The flags below each add one code; [[participant_states]] takes the codes
themselves, which is what the course rounds use to select the participants
in some states (\cref{CourseRoundParticipantsByState}).
Without any keyword we send LADOK's own default.
A list of codes counts as a keyword even when it is empty, so an empty list
is sent as it is rather than replaced by the default; the course rounds never
send one.
<<LadokSession data methods>>=
def participants_JSON(self, course_round_id: str, /, **kwargs) -> list:
  """Returns JSON record containing participants in a course identified by
  round ID.
  Filters in kwargs: not_started, ongoing, registered, finished, cancelled,
  and participant_states, a list of deltagaretillstand codes (e.g.
  ["REGISTRERAD", "AVKLARAD"]).  Returns at most PARTICIPANTS_LIMIT
  participants."""
  participants_types = list(kwargs.get("participant_states", []))
  if "not_started" in kwargs and kwargs["not_started"]:
    participants_types.append("EJ_PABORJAD")
  if "ongoing" in kwargs and kwargs["ongoing"]:
    participants_types.append("PAGAENDE")
  if "registered" in kwargs and kwargs["registered"]:
    participants_types.append("REGISTRERAD")
  if "finished" in kwargs and kwargs["finished"]:
    participants_types.append("AVKLARAD")
  if "cancelled" in kwargs and kwargs["cancelled"]:
    participants_types.append("AVBROTT")
  # 'ATERBUD', # Withdrawal
  # 'PAGAENDE_MED_SPARR', # on-going block exists
  # 'EJ_PAGAENDE_TILLFALLESBYTE', # not on-going due to instance exchange
  # 'UPPEHALL', # not on-going due to approved leave from studies

  if not kwargs:
    participants_types = ["PAGAENDE", "REGISTRERAD", "AVKLARAD"]

  put_data = {
    'page': 1,
    'limit': PARTICIPANTS_LIMIT,
    'orderby': ['EFTERNAMN_ASC',
                'FORNAMN_ASC',
                'PERSONNUMMER_ASC',
                'KONTROLLERAD_KURS_ASC'],
    'deltagaretillstand': participants_types,
    'utbildningstillfalleUID': [course_round_id]
  }

  response = self.put_query(
    '/studiedeltagande/internal/deltagare/kurstillfalle',
    put_data,
    "application/vnd.ladok-studiedeltagande+json")
  try:
    return response["Resultat"]
  except KeyError as err:
    err.add_note(f"Response data: {response}")
    raise LadokAPIError(f"Unexpected response format when fetching participants for "
                       f"round {course_round_id}: missing 'Resultat' key") from err
@

We ask for one page of at most 400 participants.
A round with more is cut off; we name the limit so that the tests can tell
when a list may be.
<<constants>>=
PARTICIPANTS_LIMIT = 400
@

Let's verify what we send, without LADOK: we call the method on a stand-in
for the session that records the request.
The codes in [[participant_states]] are sent as they are, before those of
any flags; no keyword at all sends the default, but an empty list of codes is
sent as it is.
<<test functions>>=
class SessionRecordingPuts:
  def __init__(self):
    self.sent = []

  def put_query(self, path, data, content_type):
    self.sent.append(data["deltagaretillstand"])
    return {"Resultat": []}

@pytest.mark.parametrize("kwargs,codes", [
  ({}, ["PAGAENDE", "REGISTRERAD", "AVKLARAD"]),
  ({"participant_states": ["EJ_PABORJAD", "AVBROTT"]},
   ["EJ_PABORJAD", "AVBROTT"]),
  ({"cancelled": True, "participant_states": ["AVKLARAD"]},
   ["AVKLARAD", "AVBROTT"]),
  ({"finished": True}, ["AVKLARAD"]),
  ({"participant_states": []}, []),
])
def test_participants_JSON_sends_the_codes(kwargs, codes) -> None:
  session = SessionRecordingPuts()
  assert ladok3.LadokSession.participants_JSON(session, "round", **kwargs) \
    == []
  assert session.sent == [codes]
@

