Against LADOK, we check the participant list's codes on a round of DD1317
that runs from 24 August 2026 to 11 January 2027, so that the code for the
ongoing participants is among those we ask for.
We ask for the participants of each state, with the codes that the round's
dates give for it on the day of the answers (\cref{ParticipationState}), and
for those with the one code that no state uses, [[PAGAENDE]].
What must hold on any day from the start of the round:
each participant is in at most one of these lists, and LADOK's default list
is the union of the lists of [[PAGAENDE]], [[REGISTRERAD]] and
[[AVKLARAD]], since those are the codes it defaults to, and
[[REGISTRERAD]] is ongoing or unfinished, whichever the day makes it.
No list may be full, since a full list may have been cut off
([[PARTICIPANTS_LIMIT]]).
We compare the students' LADOK IDs and assert on counts, so that a failure
shows no one.
<<test functions>>=
def test_participant_states_partition_a_round(ladok, recording,
                                              vcr) -> None:
  today = date_of_answers(recording, vcr)
  start, end = DD1317_running_round_dates

  def students(**kwargs):
    participants = ladok.participants_JSON(DD1317_running_round_id,
                                           **kwargs)
    assert len(participants) < ladok3.PARTICIPANTS_LIMIT
    return {participant["Student"]["Uid"] for participant in participants}

  by_state = {}
  for state in ladok3.COURSE_STATES:
    codes = ladok3.participant_codes(state, start, end, today)
    by_state[state] = students(participant_states=codes) if codes else set()
  pagaende = students(participant_states=["PAGAENDE"])
  default = students()

  lists = [pagaende, *by_state.values()]
  assert sum(map(len, lists)) == len(set().union(*lists))
  assert len(default ^ (pagaende | by_state["ongoing"]
                        | by_state["unfinished"]
                        | by_state["completed"])) == 0
@


\chapter{Other API calls}

The following API calls were explored and written by Chip Maguire.
Bosk has merely added tests and generated example output, as well as minor 
reformatting of the code.

\section{Code tables}\label{CodeTables}

Most of the calls in this chapter fetch one of LADOK's code tables: the
teaching languages, the municipalities, the forms of funding and so on.
They all have the same form, a dictionary with a single list of entries,
each with an [[ID]], a code ([[Kod]]) and a name ([[Benamning]]).
The [[ID]] is what other records refer to, for instance a course round's
[[StudietaktID]].
Note that the tables give the [[ID]] as a string, while the records that refer
to it use an integer, so we compare them as strings.

We test the tables with the same two checks: the table must have entries,
each with an ID and a code, and no ID may occur twice.
These hold for any version of the data in the test environment, so the tests
need not know what the tables contain.
Where a table refers to another one, its test also checks that every
reference resolves, which is what a program using the tables relies on.
<<test functions>>=
def assert_code_table(response, key):
  """Check that `response` holds a code table under `key`: a non-empty
  list of entries, each with an ID and a code, and no ID twice."""
  table = response[key]
  assert table
  assert all(entry["ID"] and entry["Kod"] for entry in table)
  ids = [entry["ID"] for entry in table]
  assert len(set(ids)) == len(ids)

def code_table_ids(response, key):
  """Return the IDs in the code table under `key`, as strings."""
  return {str(entry["ID"]) for entry in response[key]}
@

