\section{The state of a participation}\label{ParticipationState}

LADOK's views tell us the state of a participation, but only by asking.
A program that already holds a student's participations, as a [[Student]]
does once it has listed its courses, can tell the states without asking,
from two things that every participation carries:
its summary, [[TillstandKurs.Sammanfattattillstand]], a string such as
[[studiedeltagande.domain.tillstandsdefinition.registrerad]] whose last
component says how far the student has come, and the round's study period,
[[Utbildningsinformation.Studieperiod]], which together with today's date
says whether the round has started or ended.

We cannot read LADOK's rule anywhere, so we learnt it by comparison.
On 2026-10-20 we compared the five views with the summary and study period of
every participation of nine students in LADOK's test environment, 135
participations, and of the 13 participants of DD1301's 2026 rounds who had
broken off, and every one of them agreed with the following rule.
Those rounds had all started or ended, so on the same day we also sampled the
participants of a running round, DD1317's: the registered students are
[[pagaende]], those who completed are [[avslutade]], and those who have not
started are [[kommande]], as long as the round runs.
\begin{description}
\item[A withdrawal] comes first: a participation that the student withdrew
  from before the start ([[Aterbud]] is true, or
  [[TillstandKurs.Tillfallesdeltagande]] ends in [[aterbud]]) is in no state,
  whatever its summary says.
  We saw one whose summary was [[avbrott]]: the student had withdrawn, and
  LADOK listed it in no view.
\item[{[[avklarad]]}] (completed) is [[avslutade]], whatever the dates.
\item[{[[avbrott]]}] (broken off) is [[avbrott]].
\item[{[[aterbud]]}] (withdrawn before the start) and
  {[[tidigtavbrott]]} (broken off early) are in no state.
\item[{[[registrerad]]}, {[[omregistrerad]]}] (registered, or registered
  again) are [[pagaende]] while the round runs and [[oavslutade]] once it has
  ended.
\item[{[[ej_paborjad]]}] (not started) is [[kommande]] until the round has
  ended, also while it runs, and in no state once it has ended.
\end{description}
Every other case we call unknown: a registration on a round that has not
started, a summary we have not seen, a summary or study period that is
missing.
LADOK must put these somewhere, but we have not seen where, and a guess would
be wrong without anyone noticing.
The unknown state is therefore a value of its own, [[UNKNOWN_STATE]], distinct
from [[None]], which means that LADOK puts the participation in no state.
The callers can then ask LADOK when the rule does not know, and that is what
the student classes do (\cref{StudentCourses}).

The days on which a round starts or ends were not among the dates we
compared.
We take the round to run on both, which is what the words suggest, but we
have not verified it.

Whether we filter here or LADOK does, the result should be the same; we do
both for different reasons.
Filtering what we already hold costs no request, which matters when a
program lists the states of every student in a course round.
LADOK's views are the authority, though, and the only way to learn the state
of a participation that the rule cannot place.
The recorded test below keeps the two together: it compares the views with
our rule, so a change in LADOK's rule fails it in the next live run.

After the withdrawal, the rule is two tables:
one for the summaries whose state does not depend on the dates, and one for
those whose state does, keyed by the summary and where today falls relative
to the round.
A pair that is in neither table is unknown.
<<constants>>=
STATE_BY_SUMMARY = {
  "avklarad": "avslutade",
  "avbrott": "avbrott",
  "aterbud": None,
  "tidigtavbrott": None,
}

STATE_BY_SUMMARY_AND_TIMING = {
  ("registrerad", "running"): "pagaende",
  ("omregistrerad", "running"): "pagaende",
  ("registrerad", "ended"): "oavslutade",
  ("omregistrerad", "ended"): "oavslutade",
  ("ej_paborjad", "upcoming"): "kommande",
  ("ej_paborjad", "running"): "kommande",
  ("ej_paborjad", "ended"): None,
}
@

The unknown state must be one object that cannot be mistaken for a state or
for [[None]].
A string such as [[unknown]] could be taken for a state name and passed on to
LADOK; an object of a class of its own can only be compared with itself.
It survives [[pickle]] as itself, since its [[__reduce__]] names the module
attribute, so a program that pickles the states it found can still compare
them with [[is]].
The constants come before the classes in the module, so the class is defined
here, with its one instance.
<<constants>>=
class _UnknownState:
  """The state of a participation that our rule cannot place"""
  def __repr__(self):
    return "ladok3.UNKNOWN_STATE"

  def __reduce__(self):
    return "UNKNOWN_STATE"

UNKNOWN_STATE = _UnknownState()
@

[[state_of_participation]] applies the rule to a summary, a study period and
whether the student withdrew.
It takes [[today]] as an argument so that the tests can fix the date;
without it, the rule uses today's date in Sweden ([[swedish_today]] below).
The dates matter only for some summaries, so a missing date makes the state
unknown only when the rule needs it.
<<functions>>=
def state_of_participation(summary, start, end, today=None, /,
                           withdrawn=False):
  """Return the state LADOK puts a participation in.

  `summary` is TillstandKurs.Sammanfattattillstand (only its last dotted
  component matters), `start` and `end` the round's study period as
  datetime.date objects, `today` the date to judge by (default: today
  in Sweden) and `withdrawn` whether the student withdrew before the
  start (Aterbud).  Returns one of PARTICIPATION_STATES, None if LADOK
  puts the participation in no state, or UNKNOWN_STATE if the rule
  cannot tell.
  """
  if withdrawn:
    return None
  if not isinstance(summary, str):
    return UNKNOWN_STATE
  summary = summary.rsplit(".", 1)[-1]
  if summary in STATE_BY_SUMMARY:
    return STATE_BY_SUMMARY[summary]
  if start is None or end is None:
    return UNKNOWN_STATE
  timing = round_timing(start, end, today)
  return STATE_BY_SUMMARY_AND_TIMING.get((summary, timing), UNKNOWN_STATE)
@

Where today falls relative to a round is a question of its own, which is not
only about participations, so it is a function of its own.
The round runs from its first day to its last, both included (see above).
<<functions>>=
def round_timing(start, end, today=None):
  """Return "upcoming", "running" or "ended": where `today` (default:
  today in Sweden) falls relative to a round from `start` to `end`, both
  days included in the round."""
  if today is None:
    today = swedish_today()
  if today < start:
    return "upcoming"
  if today <= end:
    return "running"
  return "ended"
@

[[participation_state]] applies the rule to a participation as LADOK returns
it.
A date that is missing or not a date leaves the date [[None]], which the rule
treats as missing.
The withdrawal shows in two places, the flag [[Aterbud]] and the last
component of [[TillstandKurs.Tillfallesdeltagande]]; either one is enough.
<<functions>>=
def participation_state(participation: dict, today=None):
  """Return the state LADOK puts `participation` in, see
  state_of_participation."""
  tillstand = participation.get("TillstandKurs") or {}
  period = (participation.get("Utbildningsinformation") or {}) \
             .get("Studieperiod") or {}
  return state_of_participation(tillstand.get("Sammanfattattillstand"),
                                iso_date_or_none(period.get("Startdatum")),
                                iso_date_or_none(period.get("Slutdatum")),
                                today,
                                withdrawn=is_withdrawn(participation))

def is_withdrawn(participation: dict) -> bool:
  """Return True if the student withdrew from `participation` before
  the start (Aterbud)."""
  if participation.get("Aterbud") is True:
    return True
  tillstand = (participation.get("TillstandKurs") or {}) \
                .get("Tillfallesdeltagande")
  return isinstance(tillstand, str) \
    and tillstand.rsplit(".", 1)[-1] == "aterbud"

def iso_date_or_none(text):
  """Return the date in the ISO string `text`, or None if there is none."""
  try:
    return datetime.date.fromisoformat(text)
  except (TypeError, ValueError):
    return None
@

Which day is today?
LADOK's dates are Swedish: a round that ends on 11 January ends at midnight
in Sweden.
The machine that runs the program need not be in Sweden, and a server often
keeps UTC, which is an hour or two behind; judged by its date, a round would
still run for the first hours of the day after it ended.
So when the caller gives no date, we take today's date in Sweden.
The function takes the instant as an argument, so that the tests can fix it.
Python's [[zoneinfo]] reads the time zones from the operating system, and
Windows has none to read, so on Windows we depend on the [[tzdata]] package,
which carries them (see [[pyproject.toml]]).
<<functions>>=
def swedish_today(now=None) -> datetime.date:
  """Return the date in Sweden at the instant `now`, an aware datetime
  (default: the current instant)."""
  if now is None:
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
  return now.astimezone(zoneinfo.ZoneInfo("Europe/Stockholm")).date()
@

